## Done means verified

Before you say a change is done, run the checks for what you touched and report the
real results, including failures:

```bash
npm run lint && npm run typecheck && npm test                  # TypeScript workspaces
npm run test:stories -w @app/ui-kit                            # after UI kit changes
(cd apps/backend && uv run ruff check . && uv run ruff format --check . \
  && uv run mypy && uv run lint-imports && uv run pytest)      # backend
npm run gen:api                                                # after any API change; commit the output
```

Integration tests need Postgres: `docker compose up -d db` and
`TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://app:app@localhost:5432/app_test`.
If you couldn't run a check, say which one and why.

## Rules

**Always**

- Read the code you're changing and its tests before editing. Don't guess at an API; open it.
- Add or update tests with every behavior change.
- Backend services raise `AppError` subclasses (never `HTTPException`); request schemas
  extend `BaseSchema` and use `ShortString`/`LongString`; every query is scoped to the caller.
- Frontend UI comes from `@app/ui-kit`, colors are semantic tokens (`bg-primary`, never
  `bg-blue-600`), every user-visible string goes through i18n, and config is read only
  through `apps/frontend/src/core/config/env.ts`.
- Schema changes get an Alembic migration (`uv run alembic revision --autogenerate --rev-id NNNN`),
  reviewed by hand before committing.
- Keep changes scoped to the task. Note unrelated problems instead of fixing them silently.

**Never**

- Lower a coverage floor, skip or delete a failing test, or bypass hooks (`--no-verify`).
- Edit generated files by hand: `apps/backend/openapi.json`,
  `apps/frontend/src/core/api/schema.d.ts`, `apps/backend/src/requirements.txt`, lockfiles.
- Edit a migration that has already been applied anywhere; write a new one.
- Commit secrets, `.env` files, or real user data.
- Push to `develop`, `staging`, or `main`, or merge a PR. Open a PR and let a person merge it.

## Planning and tracking

{% if tracker == "linear" -%}
Epics and stories live in **Linear** (team key `{{ tracker_key }}`, story IDs like
`{{ tracker_key }}-12`), read and written through the Linear MCP server. A commit or PR
that finishes a story says `Closes {{ tracker_key }}-12`; earlier partial work says
`Refs {{ tracker_key }}-12`. A bare mention doesn't count.
{%- elif tracker == "github" -%}
Epics and stories are **GitHub issues** in `{{ github_repository }}` (stories carry the
`story` label; an epic issue lists its stories). A commit or PR that finishes a story
says `Closes #12`; earlier partial work says `Refs #12`.
{%- else -%}
There is no external tracker. Each plan keeps its stories in
`.implementation_plans/<plan>/STORIES.md` with IDs like `S1`, `S2`. A PR that finishes a
story says `Closes <plan>#S1` in its description; earlier partial work says `Refs`.
{%- endif %}

Multi-step features get a plan in `.implementation_plans/` (see its
[README](.implementation_plans/README.md)). The agent workflow that creates and runs
plans is described in [docs/CONTRIBUTING.md](docs/CONTRIBUTING.md).

## Commits and pull requests

- Conventional Commits: `feat:`, `fix:`, `refactor:`, `test:`, `docs:`, `chore:`.
- Commit after each logical step; don't hold a large change uncommitted.
- Fill in every section of the PR template, including the three questions.
- CI must be green. The `PR check` job is the required check.
